# ============================================================================
# Source material — the OCR text is versioned, the scans are not
# ============================================================================
# _source/ holds three OCR text files (~255 KB) and the rendered note pages
# (~12 MB of JPEGs). The OCR text is committed because the occurrence trail is
# rebuilt from it: tools/extract_occurrences.py reads
# _source/past_questions.ocr.txt to find which papers asked each question, and
# _source/note{1,2}.ocr.txt is the page index for the notes cross-check.
# (tools/render_notes.py renders from the source PDFs, not the OCR.)
#
# The page images, and the PDFs they are rendered from, are inputs only —
# roughly six times everything the site needs put together, and the PDFs live
# outside the repo — so they stay on disk, untracked. The site itself is
# self-contained: nothing under index.html, app.js, ch*.js or assets/ reads
# any of it.
_source/*
!_source/*.ocr.txt
#
# _source/dcc/ is the same idea for the second course (BCE7024). Its ten text
# files (~250 KB) are the only *readable* form of the uploaded DCC material —
# the syllabus and half the decks have no text layer, so they exist solely
# because tools/dcc_extract.py OCR'd them, and reproducing them costs a long
# run of the Windows OCR engine. That is exactly the argument for committing
# the Simulation OCR files, so they are committed too. The directory itself has
# to be re-included before its contents can be: `_source/*` matches `_source/dcc`
# as a directory, and git will not descend into an ignored directory to find a
# file that a later rule un-ignores.
!_source/dcc/

# ============================================================================
# The second course's uploaded material — input only, ~101 MB
# ============================================================================
# dcc/ is what was uploaded for BCE7024: nine lecture decks, four reference
# books (2,700 pages between them), the lab manuals and the syllabus. It is the
# input to tools/dcc_extract.py and nothing the site loads reads any of it —
# dcc-site/ loads the shared shell from the project root plus its own chapter
# files. Same reasoning as the Simulation PDFs above, so it stays on disk.
#
# The leading slash is load-bearing. A bare `dcc/` matches a directory of that
# name at ANY depth, which silently swallowed `_source/dcc/` — the extracted
# text this very file re-includes a few lines above, because the later rule
# wins. `/dcc/` means the one at the project root and nothing else.
/dcc/

# ============================================================================
# Scratch — regenerated on every run
# ============================================================================
# .freebuff/ is the coding agent's session state (project id, tmp scripts,
# .freebuff/tmp/chapters.json dumps, preview logs). .freebuff/run.md is the one
# exception: it documents how to preview a fresh clone on a free port and how
# to rebuild every generated artifact, so it is committed. Every __pycache__ is
# bytecode Python rewrites on each invocation.
.freebuff/*
!.freebuff/run.md
__pycache__/

# build_pdf.py dumps the html it hands to xhtml2pdf here so a failed run can be
# inspected. The PDF itself (Simulation_Notes.pdf) is the deliverable and is
# committed.
temp_pdf.html

# ============================================================================
# Audit snapshots
# ============================================================================
# _audit/original.sha256 hashes the frozen original and is the only integrity
# record the project has, so it stays. The sibling folders are tool-created
# pre-change copies of ch*.js — large duplicates of files git already tracks,
# and merge_past.py rewrites _audit/pre_merge/ on every --apply, so tracking
# them would dirty the tree on every merge for no diff value.
_audit/*
!_audit/original.sha256

# ============================================================================
# Deploy build — regenerated by tools/build_deploy.py
# ============================================================================
# dist/ is the shippable copy of the site: the three sheets and the seventeen
# scripts concatenated into one file each. A classic <script src> blocks the
# parser, so script N+1 was not even discovered until script N had run - the
# page was paying seventeen round trips in series rather than in parallel, which
# measured 10s to interactive over a tunnel and is a second of pure latency on
# a phone. It contains nothing that is not already tracked here, and re-running
# the script recreates it, so tracking it would add 7.5 MB of duplicates to
# every diff. Delete these two lines if you would rather commit the build and
# serve it from a folder - GitHub Pages can serve dist/ directly.
#
# dist-dcc/ and dcc-study-portal.zip are the same thing for the second course,
# built from the same shared shell. Both names are spelled out rather than
# covered by a `dist*/` pattern, because a glob that loosely aimed would also
# swallow a real folder called `distributed/` or the like.
dist/
simulation-study-portal.zip
dist-dcc/
dcc-study-portal.zip

# _site/ is the same bundle written by the Pages workflow
# (.github/workflows/pages.yml). It is a build output like dist/, and the
# artifact is uploaded from the runner rather than committed.
_site/
